    "narrative": "The Austin Waves offense faces a volatile slate today, anchored by **Kyle Tucker** (78.26) and **Andy Pages** (69.49), who represent our strongest statistical plays against Randy V\u00e1squez. Both benefit significantly from the SP skill gap, with the ATC projections favoring them heavily against a sub-optimal opposing starter. Similarly, **Jackson Merrill** (68.58) projects as a high-floor start despite a tougher matchup against Roki Sasaki; his dynamic platoon edge (+13%) and elite speed potential make him a must-start in a category where we cannot afford to leave stolen base upside on the bench. **Sam Antonacci** (65.19) also earns a strong recommendation, leveraging a prime lead-off spot against Slade Cecconi to maximize his plate appearance volume.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the lineup today was the exclusion of **Nolan Schanuel** (39.17). While he held a respectable baseline, the brutal matchup against Bryce Miller\u2014a pitcher with an elite 2.42 xERA\u2014tanked his park and skill factors, pushing him just below our \"marginal\" efficiency threshold. Consequently, we opted to roll with **Liam Hicks** (57.76) in the utility slot; his favorable park factors and platoon advantage against Michael Lorenzen provide a significantly higher ceiling for production than the defensive risk presented by the Miller matchup. We continue to monitor the status of **Jordan Westburg**, who remains sidelined on the IL, limiting our depth and increasing the pressure on our active bats to carry the load.\n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, as nearly every projection is tethered to a \"Lineup Pending\" status, introducing variance should managers shuffle the order. **Josh Lowe** (45.01) is the most precarious active play; while his platoon edge is enticing, the collision with Miller\u2019s elite arm and a park factor penalty makes him a risky start. We are essentially betting on his dynamic speed to offset the suppressed run-scoring environment. Owners should keep a close eye on the pre-game weather and official lineup cards, as any late-scratch or tactical re-ordering could force an immediate recalibration of our 5x5 targets.",
